```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.IO;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    // Number of worker threads
    const int WorkerCount = 32;

    // Blocking collection to store lines from the text file
    private static BlockingCollection<string> lines = new BlockingCollection<string>();

    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Start worker threads
        for (int i = 0; i < WorkerCount; i++)
        {
            Task.Run(() => Worker());
        }

        // Read lines from the text file
        string filePath = "path/to/your/file.txt"; // Change to your file path

        foreach (var line in File.ReadLines(filePath))
        {
            // Add lines to the collection, block if necessary based on collection limits
            lines.Add(line);
        }

        // Signal that no more items will be added
        lines.CompleteAdding();

        // Wait for all workers to finish processing
        lines.CompletionTask.Wait(); // Blocks until all lines have been processed

        Console.WriteLine("All lines processed. Exiting...");
    }

    private static async Task Worker()
    {
        foreach (var line in lines.GetConsumingEnumerable())
        {
            // Simulate processing the line
            ProcessLine(line);
        }
    }

    private static void ProcessLine(string line)
    {
        // Simulated work with a line (e.g., print to console or perform a computation)
        Console.WriteLine($"Processing line: {line}");

        // Simulate some work with a delay
        Thread.Sleep(100);  // Simulate line-processing time
    }
}
```

### Explanation:
1. **BlockingCollection**: We use `BlockingCollection<string>` to hold the lines read from the file. This collection provides thread-safe adding and taking of elements, which is crucial when dealing with multiple worker threads.

2. **Task.Run**: For each worker thread, we create a task that continuously processes elements from the `BlockingCollection` using `GetConsumingEnumerable()` method. This method automatically blocks when there are no items to process and unblocks as items become available.

3. **File Read**: We read lines from the input text file and add those lines to the `BlockingCollection`. Adding to a `BlockingCollection` will block if the internal storage is full, thus handling synchronization between the main thread and the worker threads.

4. **CompleteAdding**: After reading all lines, we call `CompleteAdding()` to signal that no more items will be added to the `BlockingCollection`. This allows the worker threads to exit once all lines have been processed.

5. **Graceful Shutdown**: Each worker will naturally exit when the `BlockingCollection` has been marked as complete and all lines have been processed.

6. **Thread Safety**: The use of `BlockingCollection` ensures that operations on the collection are thread-safe, allowing workers to independently process lines added by the main thread.

### Important Notes:
- Modify the `filePath` to point to the actual file you want to read.
- Thread.Sleep is used to simulate work and can be replaced with actual logic for processing each line.
- Make sure to handle exceptions, particularly around file access, in production code to avoid crashes.

