Creating a multi-region deployment in Azure using Terraform involves several components working in concert to enable high availability and optimized routing through Azure Traffic Manager, which will perform geo-DNS to direct traffic to the appropriate Application Gateways, eventually servicing multiple Azure API Management (APIM) backends. Let’s break down this complex deployment into various sections while providing a rich tapestry of context and explanation.

### Outline of the Infrastructure

1. **Azure Traffic Manager**: This service will manage incoming requests based on geographic location, ensuring users are directed to the nearest Application Gateway.
2. **Application Gateways**: These serve as the points of entry for incoming traffic, managing Web Application Firewall (WAF) policies and SSL termination.
3. **Azure API Management**: Backend services which provide an API gateway for managing and exposing backend services.

### Terraform Configuration

Here’s a high-level Terraform script that illustrates how to set up this architecture. Note that this script presupposes an understanding of Terraform configurations, and it will require customization to fit specific business needs or environment configurations.

```hcl
provider "azurerm" {
  features {}
}

# Define the Traffic Manager Profile
resource "azurerm_traffic_manager_profile" "my_traffic_manager" {
  name                = "myTrafficManager"
  resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.rg.name
  traffic_routing_method = "Geographic"
  dns_config {
    relative_name = "myapp"
    ttl           = 30
  }

  monitor_config {
    protocol = "HTTP"
    port     = 80
    path     = "/"
    interval_in_seconds = 30
    timeout_in_seconds  = 30
    tolerated_number_of_failures = 3
  }
}

# Resource group for Region 1
resource "azurerm_resource_group" "rg1" {
  name     = "myResourceGroup1"
  location = "East US"
}

# Application Gateway in Region 1
resource "azurerm_application_gateway" "app_gateway_rg1" {
  name                = "myApplicationGateway1"
  resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.rg1.name
  location            = azurerm_resource_group.rg1.location

  sku {
    name     = "Standard_v2"
    tier     = "Standard_v2"
    capacity = 2
  }

  gateway_ip_configuration {
    name      = "myGatewayIpConfig"
    subnet_id = azurerm_subnet.subnet_rg1.id
  }

  frontend_ip_configuration {
    name                 = "myFrontendIpConfig"
    public_ip_address_id = azurerm_public_ip.public_ip_rg1.id
  }

  backend_address_pool {
    name = "myBackendPool"
    backend_addresses {
      fqdn = azurerm_api_management.apim_rg1.gateway_url
    }
  }

  # Additional configurations for listeners, rules, etc. 
}

# Azure API Management in Region 1
resource "azurerm_api_management" "apim_rg1" {
  name                = "myAPIM1"
  resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.rg1.name
  location            = azurerm_resource_group.rg1.location
  sku {
    name     = "Developer"
    capacity = 1
  }
}

# Region 2 Definitions - similar to Region 1 as above
resource "azurerm_resource_group" "rg2" {
  name     = "myResourceGroup2"
  location = "West Europe"
}

resource "azurerm_application_gateway" "app_gateway_rg2" {
  name                = "myApplicationGateway2"
  resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.rg2.name
  location            = azurerm_resource_group.rg2.location

  sku {
    name     = "Standard_v2"
    tier     = "Standard_v2"
    capacity = 2
  }

  gateway_ip_configuration {
    name      = "myGatewayIpConfig"
    subnet_id = azurerm_subnet.subnet_rg2.id
  }

  frontend_ip_configuration {
    name                 = "myFrontendIpConfig"
    public_ip_address_id = azurerm_public_ip.public_ip_rg2.id
  }

  backend_address_pool {
    name = "myBackendPool"
    backend_addresses {
      fqdn = azurerm_api_management.apim_rg2.gateway_url
    }
  }
}

resource "azurerm_api_management" "apim_rg2" {
  name                = "myAPIM2"
  resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.rg2.name
  location            = azurerm_resource_group.rg2.location
  sku {
    name     = "Developer"
    capacity = 1
  }
}

# Configure Traffic Manager endpoints
resource "azurerm_traffic_manager_endpoint" "tn_app_gateway_rg1" {
  name                     = "appGateway1"
  resource_group_name      = azurerm_resource_group.rg1.name
  traffic_manager_profile_id = azurerm_traffic_manager_profile.my_traffic_manager.id
  resource_id              = azurerm_application_gateway.app_gateway_rg1.id
  endpoint_status          = "Enabled"
}

resource "azurerm_traffic_manager_endpoint" "tn_app_gateway_rg2" {
  name                     = "appGateway2"
  resource_group_name      = azurerm_resource_group.rg2.name
  traffic_manager_profile_id = azurerm_traffic_manager_profile.my_traffic_manager.id
  resource_id              = azurerm_application_gateway.app_gateway_rg2.id
  endpoint_status          = "Enabled"
}
```

### Explanation of Key Components

**Azure Traffic Manager Profile**: This acts as your regional traffic director. Its geo-routing policies are complex but pivotal, enabling users to be rerouted seamlessly to the nearest backend service.

**Application Gateways**: These are crucial for handling incoming requests and can be tailored with rules, routing configurations, and even WAF capabilities for increased security.

**API Management Service**: This facilitates the management of APIs, including tasks such as authentication and monitoring, while also abstracting backend complexities from the consumer.

### Conclusion

By linking these components, you construct a robust infrastructure capable of handling traffic efficiently across multiple regions. The insights gained through this setup not only enhance operational availability but also foster resilience against regional outages. This Terraform configuration serves as a foundational blueprint that can be expanded with additional considerations such as security, scalability, and compliance tailored to your specific enterprise requirements.

It's worth noting that while this script outlines the high-level necessary components, thorough testing in a non-production environment is paramount for identifying any potential configuration nuances that may arise during deployment.

